::ALG1_U06_PT_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U06_PT_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.AR.5<br><br>Question:<br>The lines \(y=x+4\) and \(y=2x+1\) intersect at \((3,7)\). What does \((3,7)\) represent? {
=A. The ordered pair that satisfies both equations#Correct. A system solution is the point that makes both equations true.
~B. The slope of both lines#The slopes are 1 and 2, not the ordered pair.
~C. The y-intercept of both lines#The y-intercepts are 4 and 1, not the ordered pair.
~D. The number of solutions#The ordered pair gives the solution, not a count of solutions.
}

::ALG1_U06_PT_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U06_PT_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.AR.6; MLA.A1.AR.7<br><br>Question:<br>A student has at most $40 to buy notebooks costing $5 each and pens costing $2 each. Which inequality models the cost constraint? {
~A. \(5n+2p \ge 40\)#This would mean the cost is at least $40, not at most $40.
=B. \(5n+2p \le 40\)#Correct. The total cost must be less than or equal to the spending limit.
~C. \(2n+5p \le 40\)#This switches the notebook and pen costs.
~D. \(5n-2p \le 40\)#Costs should be added, not subtracted.
}

::ALG1_U06_PT_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U06_PT_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.DF.1<br><br>Question:<br>Find the simple interest on $700 at 4% for 3 years. {
~A. $28#This is the interest for only one year.
~B. $84,000#This uses 4 instead of 0.04 and gives an unreasonable amount.
=C. $84#Correct. \(I=Prt=700(0.04)(3)=84\).
~D. $784#This is close to principal plus interest but uses the wrong total for the question.
}

::ALG1_U06_PT_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U06_PT_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.DF.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which expression gives the total amount for $1,000 at 5% compounded annually for 2 years? {
~A. \(1000+0.05(2)\)#This does not multiply the rate by the principal or compound.
~B. \(1000(0.05)(2)\)#This gives simple interest, not total compound amount.
~C. \(1000(1.5)^2\)#This uses 50% instead of 5%.
=D. \(1000(1.05)^2\)#Correct. Annual compounding uses growth factor 1.05 for 2 years.
}

::ALG1_U06_PT_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U06_PT_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.DF.3; MLA.A1.DF.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which display is best for comparing counts across different categories? {
=A. Bar graph#Correct. A bar graph compares counts or amounts across categories.
~B. Scatterplot#A scatterplot shows association between two numerical variables.
~C. Residual plot#A residual plot is used to examine model errors.
~D. Line of fit#A line of fit models a trend, not category counts.
}

::ALG1_U06_PT_Q06::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U06_PT_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.DF.5; MLA.A1.DF.7<br><br>Question:<br>A scatterplot rises from left to right. What does this suggest? {
~A. Negative association#A negative association falls as x increases.
=B. Positive association#Correct. As x increases, y tends to increase.
~C. No association#A visible upward trend is an association.
~D. Constant y-values#The points are changing upward, not staying constant.
}

::ALG1_U06_PT_Q07::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U06_PT_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.DF.6<br><br>Question:<br>A model predicts 68, but the actual value is 74. What is the residual? {
~A. -6#This reverses the subtraction order.
~B. 142#This adds the values instead of comparing prediction error.
=C. 6#Correct. Residual = actual - predicted = 74 - 68 = 6.
~D. 68#This is the predicted value, not the residual.
}

::ALG1_U06_PT_Q08::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U06_PT_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.DF.8<br><br>Question:<br>Which sampling method is most likely to represent an entire school fairly? {
~A. Survey only students in one club#One club may not represent the whole school.
~B. Survey only students who volunteer online#Voluntary response can overrepresent strong opinions.
~C. Survey only seniors#This leaves out other grade levels.
=D. Randomly survey students from every grade level#Correct. Random selection across grade levels better represents the population.
}

::ALG1_U06_PT_Q09::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U06_PT_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.DF.9<br><br>Question:<br>In a two-way table, 18 of 60 students in one group chose online tutoring. What is the relative frequency? {
=A. 30%#Correct. \(18/60=0.30=30\%\).
~B. 18%#This uses the count as the percent without using the total.
~C. 60%#This uses the group total as a percent.
~D. 42%#This uses the number who did not choose online tutoring.
}

::ALG1_U06_PT_Q10::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U06_PT_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.AR.5; MLA.A1.DF.8<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement best describes a simulation used before solving a real-world algebra problem? {
~A. It guarantees the exact answer before any calculation#Simulation estimates outcomes; it does not guarantee an exact answer.
=B. It can estimate likely outcomes by repeating a modeled process#Correct. Simulations use repeated trials to estimate what may happen.
~C. It replaces the need to define variables#Variables and quantities still need to be identified.
~D. It proves that every sample is unbiased#Simulation does not prove a sample was selected fairly.
}
